<!--
  Dock: navigate to a staging pose in front of the dock with move.xml's
  machinery, switch the gait to MPC, wait, hand the last metre to the
  controller's DockToPose, then lie down on the dock. Selected per goal by NavigateToGoal.behavior_tree
  ("dock.xml" -- a bare name resolves against this directory -- or the
  absolute share path); there is no separate dock action, so a dock reports
  through the same error_code / status_msg surface as a move, and the goal's
  `pose` is the DOCK pose: this tree derives the staging pose from it.

  Why a staging leg first (2026-10): the docking controller drives a straight
  line with no path and NO COSTMAP -- obstacle avoidance is switched off for
  the last metre on purpose, because the dock is itself a lethal cell in the
  local costmap and any footprint test refuses the final half metre of every
  dock -- so it must start from a pose it can reach by going straight,
  roughly in front of the dock, roughly facing it, in space the costmap-aware
  navigation has just certified clear. ComputeStagingPose derives that pose
  from the dock pose (back along the dock's yaw by `distance`), and the
  staging subtree below is move.xml's tree with goal="{staging_pose}": same
  replanning rule (replan only when the path is blocked or the goal changed),
  same retries, same attribution. Keep the two in step -- a fix to move.xml's
  shape belongs here too, and move.xml's header is the design record for
  everything inside NavigateToStaging. ComputeStagingPose sits INSIDE the
  planner branch, not before it, so a preempt that moves the dock pose (the
  navigator swaps {goal}, GlobalUpdatedGoal fires) recomputes the staging
  pose before the replan; computed once at the top it would be stale for the
  rest of the goal. The SimpleGoalChecker ends the leg within
  xy_goal_tolerance (0.15 m) and yaw_goal_tolerance (0.2 rad) of the staging
  pose; that residual is the docking controller's starting error.

  How the gait switch is checked (2026-10-14): MPC is SetMotionKey key="5"
  -> syncai_driver_manager -> `MODE M` over one-way UDP, and the driver's
  `success` means the datagram left, nothing more. The controller reports its
  motion state on the `mode` topic (std_msgs/Int32MultiArray, data[1]), and
  for MPC that is 5 (read off the robot on 2026-10-14). WaitForMotionState
  waits for it, up to `timeout` (5000 ms); a controller that never reports it
  fails the step, the Fallback restores RL, and the goal is DOCK_FAILED with
  the last state seen in error_msg. The node logs how long the switch took --
  the number to tune the timeout with. The Wait of 2000 ms after it is
  deliberate settling time: the controller reports MPC when it has entered
  the mode, not when the gait under it is steady, and a dock approach started
  mid transition is the failure this tree exists to prevent. Until 2026-10-14
  the whole check was a fixed 1500 ms Wait, a guess.

  Why the tree lies the robot down itself (2026-10-14): a dock is not done
  until the robot is resting on the contacts, and a backend LIEDOWN step
  after every DOCK was one more step to forget. LieDownOnDock sends key="2"
  (`MODE X`) once DockToPose has succeeded, then a Wait of 4000 ms -- a GUESS
  at the lie-down time, the counterpart of undock.xml's stand-up Wait, to be
  replaced by a WaitForMotionState in the same place -- so SUCCEEDED means
  "lying on the dock", not "lie-down sent". It sits OUTSIDE the DockInMpc
  Fallback: a refused lie-down (LOCKED, an unanswered call) must not run
  RestoreRL, which would switch a robot standing on the dock to the walking
  gait for nothing. Such a failure is DOCK_FAILED with SetMotionKey's reason
  and leaves the robot standing on the dock in MPC; undock.xml starts from
  there as well (its stand-up key is a no-op for a standing robot).

  Why RL is restored only on failure: on success the robot lies down on the
  dock (above), and the next step is undock.xml, which stands it up itself;
  switching back to RL first would be a step the task never asked for. On
  any failure after the switch was attempted -- the
  switch refused (LOCKED), or the approach aborted -- the Fallback's second
  child sends key="1" (`MODE C`, RL locomotion) so the robot is left as the
  step found it, and ForceFailure keeps the goal a failure. The Fallback
  wraps only the MPC half: a staging-leg failure (no route, path blocked)
  never switched the gait, so it needs no restore, and its attribution --
  PLAN_FAILED / FOLLOW_PATH_FAILED, "the dock was unreachable" -- is more
  useful than DOCK_FAILED would be. A CANCEL during the approach halts the
  tree and cannot run the restore (halt() does not tick), so a cancelled dock
  leaves the gait in MPC, standing; the backend restores RL itself before
  the next MOVE.

  Attribution (last writer wins, see move.xml and blackboard_keys.hpp): the
  staging subtree writes ComputePathToPose / FollowPath as in move.xml;
  SetMotionKey writes itself on a refused key or an unanswered call;
  WaitForMotionState writes itself when MPC is never reported; DockToPose
  writes itself with the controller's reason. The navigator maps the last
  three (and ComputeStagingPose) to DOCK_FAILED. One accepted
  consequence: when the dock approach fails AND the restore is refused
  (LOCKED), the restore's SetMotionKey is the last writer, so error_msg says
  LOCKED and the approach's reason is in the task_runner log at ERROR
  (DockToPose::on_aborted logs it). That is the right order of importance: a
  locked driver stops every later step, the dock reason does not.

  Status sentence: the staging leg writes nav_status_msg as move.xml does
  ("Planning a path to the goal" from the navigator, "Following the path",
  the replanning / retry sentences). SetBlackboard then says the gait is
  switching -- written by the tree, not by SetMotionKey, which is a generic
  node that does not know why it is sending key 5 -- and DockToPose writes
  "Approaching the dock", "Docking: 0.42 m from the dock" from the
  controller's feedback, and "Docked" / "Dock approach failed"; the tree's
  last sentence is "Docked; lying down". output_key is
  the BARE key on purpose: with {nav_status_msg} SetBlackboard would read the
  current sentence and use it as the key name. During the approach the
  navigator's distance_remaining is still measured along the staging path
  (the robot stands at its end, so it reads <= 0.15 m); the dock distance is
  in status_msg only.

  server_timeout="500" on every SetMotionKey call: the default 20 ms is the
  action-ack budget, but for a service it is the whole call, and the
  driver's four services share one worker thread, so a set_speed_scale in
  flight from the console would time the switch out. Half a second is still
  far below anything a human would notice.

  Not a RecoveryNode around DockToPose: a dock approach that failed is
  re-tried by re-sending the goal, i.e. by the backend, with the robot
  standing somewhere in the last metre; a blind in-tree retry from a pose the
  controller already gave up on has no new information.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <Sequence name="Dock">
      <!-- Staging leg: move.xml with goal="{staging_pose}"; see move.xml for the why -->
      <PipelineSequence name="NavigateToStaging">
        <RateController hz="1.0">
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
            <Fallback name="FallbackComputePathToPose">
              <ReactiveSequence name="CheckIfNewPathNeeded">
                <Inverter>
                  <GlobalUpdatedGoal/>
                </Inverter>
                <IsPathValid path="{path}" server_timeout="100" blocked_time="10.0"/>
              </ReactiveSequence>
              <Sequence name="PlanToStaging">
                <ComputeStagingPose dock_pose="{goal}" distance="1.0" staging_pose="{staging_pose}"/>
                <ComputePathToPose goal="{staging_pose}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
              </Sequence>
            </Fallback>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Context" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </RateController>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="4" retry_refill_time="60.0" name="FollowPath">
          <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
      </PipelineSequence>

      <!-- MPC half: restore RL on any failure after the switch was attempted -->
      <Fallback name="DockInMpc">
        <Sequence name="SwitchAndDock">
          <SetBlackboard output_key="nav_status_msg" value="Switching the gait to MPC for docking"/>
          <SetMotionKey key="5" server_timeout="500"/>
          <!-- Confirm the controller reports MPC (mode data[1] == 5), then let it settle (header) -->
          <WaitForMotionState state="5" timeout="5000"/>
          <Wait msec="2000"/>
          <DockToPose dock_pose="{goal}" controller_id="Docking" goal_checker_id="dock_goal_checker" timeout="60.0"/>
        </Sequence>
        <ForceFailure>
          <SetMotionKey name="RestoreRL" key="1" server_timeout="500"/>
        </ForceFailure>
      </Fallback>

      <!-- On the dock: lie down. Outside DockInMpc on purpose -- a refused
           lie-down must not switch a robot standing on the dock to RL (header) -->
      <Sequence name="LieDownOnDock">
        <SetBlackboard output_key="nav_status_msg" value="Docked; lying down"/>
        <SetMotionKey name="LieDown" key="2" server_timeout="500"/>
        <!-- Lie-down time: a guess until measured, like undock.xml's stand-up (header) -->
        <Wait msec="4000"/>
      </Sequence>
    </Sequence>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
